# Who may compose the layer dimension, and whether anybody else does. One row per law, with the
# templates that compose `layers/every_layer.sqlc` and are not declared as denominators.
#
# A dimension reaches everything
#   It does so by construction, so composing it destroys every bound downstream.
#   `layers/every_layer.sqlc` is every filed layer, which is what makes it the denominator a
#   `LEFT JOIN` can find a gap against. The moment a relation composes it for any other reason,
#   every consumer of that relation inherits the whole dimension, and any law of the form *this
#   rule's reach is inside the reach of what it composes* becomes vacuous.
#
# One edge is enough
#   `composition/parts.sqlc` is composed by more relations than anything else here, so an inner
#   join of the dimension there puts the whole dimension into the closure of most of the checker.
#   A membership test reads `pm.layer`; the dimension is for denominators. Same rows, opposite
#   roles, and only one of the two is safe to propagate.
#
# The three roles
#   A blanket rule would forbid something correct. A denominator drives the query or is
#   left-joined, and that is what this relation permits. A membership test asks whether one pair
#   exists and belongs on the base table. And `entries/spillovers.sqlc` is a third: it self-joins
#   the dimension with `filing <> filing` to find the same layer name in another filing, so its
#   subject genuinely is the whole dimension. That is why the permitted set is declared here
#   rather than inferred from the join direction, which the compose DAG cannot see.
#
# What it cannot see
#   It cannot see how a permitted parent uses the dimension, only that it is permitted. So a file
#   on this list that later starts inner-joining the dimension passes, and the law's reach is the
#   boundary of the list rather than of the usage. The join direction is in the SQL text, so the
#   program that reads the text classifies it and carries it in
#   `public.compose_edge.inner_joins`, which is what keeps this list to the genuine exceptions
#   rather than to every permitted parent.
#
# One exception, by name
#   `inner_joins` carries the syntax, so the list is one row. Permitting parents by name would
#   mean adding every ordinary denominator use by hand, and a list like that rots on changes that
#   are fine. Driving from the dimension or left-joining it passes without being declared; only an
#   inner join needs an argument. `entries/spillovers.sqlc` is the one that has one, for the
#   reason under the three roles: the whole dimension genuinely is its subject rather than a
#   lookup.
-- algebra/roster.sqlc against public.compose_edge: who composes the layer dimension.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'layers/every_layer.sqlc' AS subject,
           count(*) = 0              AS holds,
           format('%s undeclared parent(s) compose the layer dimension: %s',
                  count(*), coalesce(string_agg(u.parent, ', ' ORDER BY u.parent), '(none)'))
                                     AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT e.parent
        FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_edges.sqlc) ) e
        WHERE e.child = 'layers/every_layer.sqlc'
          AND e.inner_joins > 0
          AND e.parent NOT IN ('entries/spillovers.sqlc')
    ) u
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'dimension_use'
